  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Texas building codes are not one rulebook handed down from Austin. They are mostly adopted and enforced locally, which means the requirements for your new home depend on which city, extraterritorial jurisdiction or county your lot sits in. Once you understand who sets the rules and what inspectors actually check, the permitting process stops feeling like a maze and starts feeling like a checklist.</p>

<h2>Who actually sets the building code in Texas</h2>
<p>Most Texas cities base their residential rules on the <strong>International Residential Code (IRC)</strong>, published by the International Code Council, and then layer on local amendments. State law points cities toward the IRC as the baseline for one- and two-family homes, but each city decides which edition to adopt and what to change. Energy rules work the same way: Texas sets a minimum energy code, and some cities go further.</p>
<p>That is why a framing detail that passes in one town can get flagged twenty miles away. Before you finalize plans, find out:</p>
<ul>
<li>Which edition of the IRC (and the companion electrical, plumbing, mechanical and energy codes) your city has adopted.</li>
<li>What local amendments apply, especially for wind, foundations, drainage and energy.</li>
<li>Whether your lot is inside city limits, in the city's extraterritorial jurisdiction (ETJ), or in unincorporated county land.</li>
</ul>
<p>Code editions are updated every few years, so always confirm the current edition and fees with the local permitting office rather than relying on an old blog post or a builder's memory.</p>

<h3>What about land outside city limits?</h3>
<p>This is where Texas surprises people. Counties generally have much less authority over residential construction than cities do, so in many unincorporated areas you will not find a city-style building department doing plan review and inspections. State law does set some baseline expectations for new homes, and counties still handle things like septic (on-site sewage) permits, floodplain development permits and driveway or culvert permits. Do not read "no building department" as "no rules." Lenders and insurers will often still want a code-compliant house, and many owners hire third-party inspectors anyway.</p>

<h2>The code issues that matter most in Texas</h2>
<p>Texas is big enough to have several climates, and local amendments tend to follow the risks each region faces.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Concern</th><th>Where it bites hardest</th><th>What codes typically address</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Wind</td><td>Gulf Coast, Panhandle</td><td>Roof sheathing nailing, hurricane straps and ties, window and door ratings</td></tr>
<tr><td>Expansive soils</td><td>Dallas-Fort Worth, Austin, San Antonio</td><td>Engineered slab or pier-and-beam designs, soil reports, drainage away from the foundation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flooding</td><td>Houston, coastal and river corridors</td><td>Finished floor elevation, flood-resistant materials, floodplain permits</td></tr>
<tr><td>Heat and energy</td><td>Statewide</td><td>Insulation levels, air sealing, duct leakage testing, window performance, HVAC sizing</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wildfire</td><td>Hill Country, West Texas edges</td><td>Roof coverings, vents and, in some areas, defensible space</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>If you are building on clay soil, budget for a geotechnical report and an engineer-designed foundation. It is one of the best dollars you will spend. Energy rules deserve the same attention: insulation levels, air sealing and duct testing are now routine inspection items in many Texas cities.</p>

<h2>How the permitting process usually works</h2>
<p>Every city has its own portal and forms, but the sequence is similar almost everywhere:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Zoning and site check.</strong> Confirm setbacks, height limits, impervious cover and any deed restrictions or HOA rules.</li>
<li><strong>Plan submittal.</strong> Submit a site plan, floor plans, elevations, structural and foundation details, and an energy compliance report.</li>
<li><strong>Plan review.</strong> Reviewers send comments. You or your designer revise and resubmit until the plans are approved.</li>
<li><strong>Permits issued.</strong> The building permit is issued, and trade permits (electrical, plumbing, mechanical) are pulled by licensed contractors.</li>
<li><strong>Inspections.</strong> Inspectors check work at set stages before it gets covered up.</li>
<li><strong>Final and certificate of occupancy.</strong> Once everything passes, the city signs off and you can move in.</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Print the city's inspection list for your permit type and tape it inside your job binder. Before every pour or cover-up, walk the site against that list yourself. Catching a missing strap or nail plate before the inspector does saves a reinspection fee and a lost day.</p></div>
<p>A plain-language code reference helps a lot when you are reading reviewer comments or walking the job. Books like [[amz:books_code_check|Code Check]] translate the IRC into illustrated checklists that owner-builders and new supers find easy to use.</p>
<!--products:books_code_check,books_code_check_electrical,home_planner|Code references worth keeping in your job binder-->

<h2>Typical inspections on a new Texas home</h2>
<p>The names and order vary by city, but expect something close to this:</p>
<ul>
<li>Plumbing underground (before the slab is poured)</li>
<li>Foundation or pre-pour (forms, steel or post-tension cables, vapor barrier)</li>
<li>Framing, plus rough electrical, plumbing and mechanical</li>
<li>Insulation and, in many cities, energy testing such as blower door and duct leakage</li>
<li>Final building and trade finals</li>
</ul>
<p>Failed inspections are normal. What hurts is failing the same item twice. Read the correction notice carefully, fix it fully, and ask the inspector a respectful question if something is unclear.</p>

<h2>Licensing: who can do what</h2>
<p>Texas does not license general contractors for residential work at the state level, but it does license key trades. Electricians and HVAC contractors are licensed by the <strong>Texas Department of Licensing and Regulation (TDLR)</strong>, and plumbers by the <strong>Texas State Board of Plumbing Examiners</strong>. In most cities, trade permits must be pulled by those licensed pros. Many cities let a homeowner pull the building permit on a house they will live in, but check before you plan around that.</p>
<p>If you are thinking about running the project yourself, read <a href="/how-to-be-your-own-general-contractor/">how to be your own general contractor</a> before you commit. Verify every trade license online before work starts; it takes two minutes and protects your permit, your insurance and your house.</p>

<h2>City-by-city guides</h2>
<p>The big four metros each run their own departments and portals. Start with the guide for your area:</p>
